{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"Verified from GET /api/v1/releases/current (HTTP 200): the current stable SCOPe release is version 2.08, reported with stable_label "2.08-stable" (freeze date 2021-07-28, release date 2021-09-22, is_public true, is_comprehensive false, old_version null). The latest periodic update reported is version "2.08-2026-08-16", dated 2026-08-16. The release-scoped API base for it is /api/v1/releases/2.08.

Identifier to cite for reproducible analysis: the stable public release selector, SCOPe 2.08 (stable label 2.08-stable), used as the {release} path segment in all release-scoped requests (e.g., /api/v1/releases/2.08/...). Stable releases are the normal basis for reproducible work; a periodic update does not replace the stable release and should not be substituted for it as the citation. If an analysis specifically depended on records present only in a periodic update, cite the stable release and additionally record the exact periodic label (2.08-2026-08-16) and the query date, because the periodic label is a moving target that will be superseded by later updates.

What the periodic label means: "2.08-2026-08-16" is a dated incremental update layered on top of the frozen 2.08 stable release, indicating the date (2026-08-16) through which additional (typically automated) classifications of newer PDB entries have been added to the 2.08 framework without changing the stable hierarchy. Limits: the current-status response reports only labels and dates. It does not itself enumerate which records the periodic update added, nor whether any particular classification changed, so those specifics cannot be asserted from this endpoint alone; they would require querying the specific domains or releases involved. Note that is_comprehensive is false, meaning 2.08 does not claim to classify every PDB entry.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases/current","scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md"]}]}
